 ## Overview
-Multi-view Consistency Depth Filtering is a mechanism to verify the accuracy and reliability of monocular depth estimates before distilling them into a radiance field [S1, unofficial]. It generates a binary confidence map by measuring the consistency between scaled monocular depth predictions and rendered NeRF depth across multiple viewpoints, filtering out inaccurate rays [S1, unofficial]. It differs from sibling methods by explicitly leveraging the geometric consistency of the learned NeRF itself as a ground-truth proxy to mask noisy monocular depth, rather than relying solely on static thresholds or external consistency checks [S1, unofficial].
